        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best type of stain for a concrete patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For an even, single colour, a water-based penetrating or semi-transparent concrete stain is the easiest to control and works on most sound patios. Choose acid stain only if you want a variegated, natural-stone look and your slab reacts well in a test. Use a solid-colour stain when the patio has patches or mismatched concrete you need to hide."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you need to etch concrete before staining?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For many water-based and acrylic stains, yes. Behr's semi-transparent stain instructions call for etching to a profile like 150-grit sandpaper, then a water test: if water is absorbed quickly, the surface is ready. Dense, trowelled concrete that beads water with no sealer on it needs etching or light grinding before any stain will take evenly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long after pouring can you stain a concrete patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Labels typically ask for about 30 days. Behr specifies a minimum 30-day cure for its semi-transparent concrete stain, and Direct Colors says a new slab must cure 30 days before acid staining and should look uniformly light grey. Staining sooner risks blotchy colour because the slab is still releasing moisture unevenly."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I have to neutralise concrete stain?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only acid stain. Acid stain leaves the surface acidic and coated in residue, so it must be neutralised, scrubbed, rinsed and vacuumed until the rinse water runs clear before sealing. Water-based, semi-transparent and solid-colour stains do not react with the concrete and need no neutralising; just let them dry for the time on the label."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should you seal a stained concrete patio?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. The sealer protects the colour from wear, weather and UV and brings out its depth. Use two thin coats of a sealer compatible with your stain, and add non-slip grit on steps, slopes and around water. Behr advises against using its wet-look and low-lustre sealers around pool decks or constantly wet areas."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many coats of stain does a concrete patio need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually one or two. Behr notes that a second coat of its semi-transparent stain gives a darker and more uniform colour, with about 4 hours between coats. Let the first coat dry before deciding, and test the exact number of coats on a hidden patch first, since porosity changes how much colour each coat adds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does stained concrete last outdoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A true penetrating or acid stain does not peel and lasts as long as the surface it is in, provided the sealer over it is maintained. Semi-transparent acrylic and solid stains behave more like thin coatings and can wear in traffic paths. In all cases, the sealer is the part that wears first, so recoat it when water stops beading."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
